nodecode-shodan is a rewrite of Adolanium's Adolanium/hermes-plugin-shodan:

  https://github.com/Adolanium/hermes-plugin-shodan
  commit d12828e9c90654bca598d45832cee2ef6454cf30
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Adolanium (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the twelve model-facing tools and their argument schemas
(shodan_host, shodan_search, shodan_count, shodan_dns, shodan_cve,
shodan_account, shodan_meta, shodan_scan, shodan_alert, shodan_exploits,
shodan_query, shodan_trends), the five API families they reach
(api.shodan.io, exploits.shodan.io, trends.shodan.io and the two keyless
ones, internetdb.shodan.io and cvedb.shodan.io), the response shaping that
keeps a busy host inside the context window at three verbosities, the
free-count-first credit discipline with a per-session ledger that refuses
rather than overspending, the hostname resolution and the InternetDB
fallback, the scan guards (off by default, an allowlist, one credit per
address) and the alert actions, and the /shodan command's forgiving
dispatch (a bare address is a host lookup, anything else a free count with
facets). The Python (httpx, threading, dataclasses) is Common Lisp here:
nlk:http is the one seam, one section declaration carries the settings and
twelve nlk:define-contract declarations carry the tools' arguments, so the
schema the model is shown and the body that runs are one shape.

Left out: the core/full tool profile split, because a cell registers the
tools it declares and all twelve are declared; the YAML config file under
~/.hermes and the `hermes shodan` CLI (setup, doctor, profile full), whose
work a cell's /settings and /shodan command already do; the bundled skills
(query-syntax, recon, monitoring) as separate skill files, whose method is
folded into the manual the twelve tools carry; and the per-thread rate
limiter, replaced by a single paced seam with a small TTL cache, since one
process makes the calls here.
